1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,360
These are photographs from Mars.

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:07,720
Ten small titanium tubes, lying on the
ground, exactly where a robot put them.

3
00:00:08,226 --> 00:00:10,946
Inside each one is a core of Martian rock,

4
00:00:11,026 --> 00:00:14,006
drilled and sealed
by NASA's Perseverance rover.

5
00:00:14,701 --> 00:00:18,221
And one of the tubes still on the rover
holds something unusual.

6
00:00:18,601 --> 00:00:22,561
A rock with tiny dark spots,
like the coat of a leopard.

7
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:26,560
The spots are made of two minerals
that, on Earth, often form

8
00:00:26,790 --> 00:00:30,320
where microbes feed on mud
and decaying organic matter.

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:35,580
In 2025, scientists described it
as a potential biosignature.

10
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,900
Not proof of life.

11
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,120
A pattern that could have
a biological origin.

12
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:44,540
The rover can tell what the spots
are made of.

13
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,200
Not how they formed.

14
00:00:46,913 --> 00:00:50,513
To find out what it really is,
the rock has to come to Earth.

15
00:00:51,169 --> 00:00:53,449
NASA had a plan to bring it here.

16
00:00:54,149 --> 00:00:58,289
Less than half a kilogram of rock,
for an estimated eleven billion dollars.

17
00:00:59,331 --> 00:01:02,291
That's more than twenty million dollars
for every gram.

18
00:01:02,647 --> 00:01:07,307
And in January 2026,
the original programme lost its funding.

19
00:01:08,134 --> 00:01:10,794
Which is strange,
because we've done this before.

20
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:16,774
In 1969, Apollo 11 brought back
about twenty-two kilograms of the Moon.

21
00:01:17,041 --> 00:01:18,901
And not only with astronauts.

22
00:01:19,801 --> 00:01:24,801
In 2024, a Chinese robot brought back
samples from the far side of the Moon.

23
00:01:25,417 --> 00:01:29,757
So why can we bring rocks back from the
Moon, but not half a kilogram from Mars?

24
00:01:32,427 --> 00:01:34,367
Start with the obvious difference.

25
00:01:35,187 --> 00:01:36,387
Mars is bigger.

26
00:01:37,027 --> 00:01:40,270
Its gravity is more than twice
as strong as the Moon's.

27
00:01:40,569 --> 00:01:44,729
To escape the Moon, you need about
two point four kilometres per second.

28
00:01:44,889 --> 00:01:47,589
To escape Mars, about five.

29
00:01:48,083 --> 00:01:51,583
That sounds like twice the effort.
It's much worse.

30
00:01:52,341 --> 00:01:56,221
If you've seen our video on getting
to orbit, you know the rocket equation.

31
00:01:57,067 --> 00:01:59,387
Fuel doesn't grow in step with speed.

32
00:02:00,227 --> 00:02:04,767
It grows exponentially, because extra fuel
has to lift more fuel, too.

33
00:02:05,335 --> 00:02:09,815
In a simple example, a small rocket
leaving the Moon can be about half fuel.

34
00:02:10,395 --> 00:02:14,415
Leaving Mars, with the same kind
of engine, about three quarters.

35
00:02:15,719 --> 00:02:18,119
And that rocket has to get there first.

36
00:02:18,779 --> 00:02:22,039
Everything that launches from Mars
has to land on Mars.

37
00:02:22,771 --> 00:02:25,031
The Moon has no atmosphere at all.

38
00:02:25,771 --> 00:02:28,371
Mars has a thin one,
just enough to burn you up,

39
00:02:28,601 --> 00:02:31,511
and too little for parachutes
to stop you on their own.

40
00:02:32,043 --> 00:02:33,423
NASA's planned lander would have weighed

41
00:02:33,653 --> 00:02:35,983
about two thousand three hundred
kilograms.

42
00:02:36,063 --> 00:02:38,423
More than twice Perseverance.

43
00:02:38,683 --> 00:02:41,723
It would have been the heaviest thing
ever landed on Mars.

44
00:02:42,466 --> 00:02:43,826
Then it has to wait.

45
00:02:44,166 --> 00:02:49,086
Through dust storms, and nights of minus
eighty degrees, for the samples to arrive.

46
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:53,280
The best launch windows
come roughly every twenty-six months.

47
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,160
Miss one,
and the next is more than two years away.

48
00:02:57,609 --> 00:03:00,249
And then it has to launch,
with no one there.

49
00:03:00,969 --> 00:03:04,869
A radio signal from Earth takes
between three and twenty-two minutes.

50
00:03:05,579 --> 00:03:08,819
No rocket has ever launched
from the surface of another planet.

51
00:03:09,579 --> 00:03:10,959
The Moon isn't a planet.

52
00:03:11,639 --> 00:03:14,299
A rocket launched from Mars
would be the first.

53
00:03:16,989 --> 00:03:18,469
So is it worth it?

54
00:03:19,709 --> 00:03:22,869
After all,
we already have pieces of Mars on Earth.

55
00:03:23,538 --> 00:03:27,978
In 2025, the largest one ever found
was sold at auction in New York.

56
00:03:28,208 --> 00:03:32,378
Twenty-four and a half kilograms
of Martian rock.

57
00:03:33,001 --> 00:03:35,621
It sold for five point
three million dollars.

58
00:03:35,701 --> 00:03:38,421
About two hundred dollars a gram.

59
00:03:39,115 --> 00:03:42,455
A hundred thousand times cheaper
than the rock NASA wanted.

60
00:03:42,935 --> 00:03:45,235
So why not study meteorites?

61
00:03:46,291 --> 00:03:48,651
Because a meteorite has no address.

62
00:03:49,816 --> 00:03:53,116
It was blasted off Mars by an impact,
drifted through space

63
00:03:53,346 --> 00:03:56,236
for millions of years,
and burned through our atmosphere.

64
00:03:56,737 --> 00:04:00,897
Then it lay in a desert.
We don't know where on Mars it came from,

65
00:04:00,977 --> 00:04:04,137
what the impact did to it,
or what Earth has added since.

66
00:04:04,857 --> 00:04:06,917
That last part is not a detail.

67
00:04:07,857 --> 00:04:11,457
In 1996, scientists announced
possible fossil microbes

68
00:04:11,687 --> 00:04:14,277
in a Martian meteorite from Antarctica.

69
00:04:15,216 --> 00:04:17,276
It made headlines around the world.

70
00:04:17,876 --> 00:04:19,716
The debate lasted for decades.

71
00:04:20,556 --> 00:04:23,376
And it had spent thirteen thousand years
in Antarctic ice,

72
00:04:23,606 --> 00:04:26,756
long enough for Earth
to leave traces of its own.

73
00:04:29,016 --> 00:04:31,996
The rocks in Perseverance's tubes
are the opposite.

74
00:04:32,756 --> 00:04:35,676
We know exactly which layer
each one came from.

75
00:04:36,197 --> 00:04:38,497
And a rover can only do so much.

76
00:04:38,957 --> 00:04:41,817
Its instruments had to fit
on a robot the size of a car,

77
00:04:42,047 --> 00:04:44,137
and survive a rocket launch.

78
00:04:44,871 --> 00:04:48,331
On Earth, the samples could go to
any laboratory in the world.

79
00:04:48,411 --> 00:04:52,691
Including ones with instruments
that haven't been invented yet.

80
00:04:53,353 --> 00:04:57,573
The rocks from Apollo are still being
studied, more than fifty years later.

81
00:04:58,206 --> 00:05:00,046
Some were kept sealed on purpose.

82
00:05:01,006 --> 00:05:05,086
In 2022, NASA opened a tube
that astronauts had closed on the Moon

83
00:05:05,316 --> 00:05:08,666
in 1972, waiting for better tools.

84
00:05:11,526 --> 00:05:13,986
So how did NASA plan to do it?

85
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:17,906
A rover would collect the tubes
and bring them to the lander.

86
00:05:18,686 --> 00:05:22,846
A small rocket would carry them up,
in a container the size of a basketball.

87
00:05:23,559 --> 00:05:26,039
That rocket would weigh
a few hundred kilograms.

88
00:05:26,479 --> 00:05:29,679
The rock inside,
less than half a kilogram.

89
00:05:30,070 --> 00:05:31,770
But it wouldn't fly home.

90
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:34,450
It would only reach orbit around Mars.

91
00:05:35,267 --> 00:05:38,827
There, a second spacecraft,
to be built by the European Space Agency,

92
00:05:39,057 --> 00:05:43,127
would have to find the container,
catch it, and carry it to Earth.

93
00:05:43,768 --> 00:05:45,828
Why not just fly straight home?

94
00:05:46,973 --> 00:05:49,713
A direct return would need
a much bigger rocket,

95
00:05:49,953 --> 00:05:53,193
with enough fuel to escape Mars
and head towards Earth.

96
00:05:53,993 --> 00:05:58,133
And that whole rocket, fuel and all,
would have to land on Mars first.

97
00:05:58,847 --> 00:06:01,007
Apollo faced the same choice.

98
00:06:01,467 --> 00:06:06,587
It left its return engine waiting in lunar
orbit, and sent only a small lander down.

99
00:06:08,777 --> 00:06:11,097
It's often imagined as catching a bullet.

100
00:06:11,917 --> 00:06:13,117
It isn't.

101
00:06:13,317 --> 00:06:17,997
Both spacecraft circle Mars together,
barely moving relative to each other.

102
00:06:18,541 --> 00:06:20,661
The hard part is everything before.

103
00:06:21,241 --> 00:06:22,941
The container doesn't steer.

104
00:06:23,661 --> 00:06:26,921
The orbiter has to find it,
alone, in a very large sky.

105
00:06:27,244 --> 00:06:31,404
Then approach and grab it,
with no one on Earth able to step in.

106
00:06:31,969 --> 00:06:35,669
A lander, a rover, a rocket,
an orbiter, a capture system.

107
00:06:36,289 --> 00:06:38,389
Each one has to work the first time.

108
00:06:40,089 --> 00:06:43,109
So the estimates kept climbing,
review after review.

109
00:06:43,189 --> 00:06:46,449
Around four billion dollars in 2020.

110
00:06:47,049 --> 00:06:48,309
Later, seven.

111
00:06:48,389 --> 00:06:52,909
Then eleven, with the samples arriving
no sooner than 2040.

112
00:06:53,439 --> 00:06:55,519
Cheaper versions were drawn up.

113
00:06:55,899 --> 00:06:57,399
None of them was funded.

114
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:02,266
So what if we skipped all the handovers?

115
00:07:03,206 --> 00:07:07,046
In 2024, NASA asked companies
for new ideas.

116
00:07:07,906 --> 00:07:11,066
One of the seven studies
it paid for came from SpaceX.

117
00:07:12,072 --> 00:07:15,652
Its title was
Enabling Mars Sample Return With Starship.

118
00:07:16,435 --> 00:07:18,435
SpaceX shared few details.

119
00:07:18,515 --> 00:07:21,115
But the appeal is easy to see.

120
00:07:21,735 --> 00:07:25,255
Instead of several smaller machines,
one very large spacecraft.

121
00:07:26,135 --> 00:07:28,915
Imagine a ship big enough
to combine several of these jobs.

122
00:07:29,145 --> 00:07:33,775
Landing, collecting samples,
and eventually bringing them home.

123
00:07:34,295 --> 00:07:37,575
But a ship that size
needs a lot of fuel to leave Mars.

124
00:07:38,395 --> 00:07:40,015
Far too much to bring along.

125
00:07:40,529 --> 00:07:42,749
So the idea is to make it there.

126
00:07:43,409 --> 00:07:45,949
The atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide.

127
00:07:46,749 --> 00:07:49,809
Add hydrogen from water ice,
and you can make methane.

128
00:07:50,655 --> 00:07:54,035
Oxygen can be pulled
from the carbon dioxide, too.

129
00:07:54,655 --> 00:07:57,195
Perseverance has already tried that part.

130
00:07:57,895 --> 00:08:01,075
A small device called MOXIE
made oxygen on Mars.

131
00:08:01,836 --> 00:08:06,236
Over two years, in sixteen runs,
it made a hundred and twenty-two grams.

132
00:08:07,076 --> 00:08:09,776
About what a small dog breathes
in ten hours.

133
00:08:10,403 --> 00:08:11,223
Now take a Starship

134
00:08:11,453 --> 00:08:13,763
with around twelve hundred tonnes
of propellant,

135
00:08:13,843 --> 00:08:15,723
the figure usually quoted.

136
00:08:16,473 --> 00:08:18,173
Most of that is oxygen.

137
00:08:18,253 --> 00:08:21,007
More than nine hundred tonnes.

138
00:08:21,307 --> 00:08:24,447
That's more than seven million times
what MOXIE made.

139
00:08:25,093 --> 00:08:27,093
This isn't a test of Starship.

140
00:08:27,713 --> 00:08:30,093
MOXIE was never meant to fuel anything.

141
00:08:30,653 --> 00:08:32,373
It proved the chemistry works.

142
00:08:33,087 --> 00:08:35,907
The gap is between a demonstration
and a factory.

143
00:08:36,627 --> 00:08:38,867
And nobody has built that factory yet.

144
00:08:41,277 --> 00:08:43,497
And even when the samples
are on their way,

145
00:08:43,577 --> 00:08:46,077
the hardest decision may still be ahead.

146
00:08:46,749 --> 00:08:48,269
How do you bring them down?

147
00:08:49,659 --> 00:08:53,079
Scientists don't expect
anything alive in those tubes.

148
00:08:53,819 --> 00:08:56,079
But nobody can prove there isn't.

149
00:08:56,341 --> 00:08:57,921
So the rules are strict.

150
00:08:58,721 --> 00:09:02,261
Nothing from Mars should reach Earth
outside a sealed container.

151
00:09:02,881 --> 00:09:04,901
And nothing from Earth should get in.

152
00:09:05,243 --> 00:09:07,683
That second part matters just as much.

153
00:09:08,403 --> 00:09:10,963
Even a trace of Earth life
could cloud the very question

154
00:09:11,193 --> 00:09:12,963
the mission was built to answer.

155
00:09:14,863 --> 00:09:18,603
In NASA's original design,
the capsule had no parachute.

156
00:09:19,439 --> 00:09:22,139
A parachute is one more thing
that can fail.

157
00:09:22,879 --> 00:09:26,599
So the capsule would simply fall
into soft ground in the Utah desert,

158
00:09:26,824 --> 00:09:30,144
at about a hundred and forty-five
kilometres an hour.

159
00:09:30,724 --> 00:09:33,684
Built to survive the impact
with the container intact.

160
00:09:34,581 --> 00:09:37,161
And then the samples would go
into a high-containment laboratory,

161
00:09:37,391 --> 00:09:40,561
to be checked before anyone else
could touch them.

162
00:09:43,351 --> 00:09:45,451
So where does that leave us now?

163
00:09:46,313 --> 00:09:49,613
In November 2026, NASA took another look.

164
00:09:49,693 --> 00:09:52,453
Not with a mission, but with a team.

165
00:09:53,067 --> 00:09:54,887
It starts work in January.

166
00:09:55,627 --> 00:09:59,007
Its recommendations are due
around the end of 2027.

167
00:09:59,487 --> 00:10:02,227
There's no launch date,
and no mission budget.

168
00:10:04,007 --> 00:10:06,987
Meanwhile, China is building
a mission of its own.

169
00:10:07,427 --> 00:10:08,627
It's called Tianwen-3.

170
00:10:09,695 --> 00:10:12,055
Two rockets, around 2028.

171
00:10:12,355 --> 00:10:14,575
A lander with a small ascent rocket.

172
00:10:15,115 --> 00:10:19,373
An orbiter waiting above.
A handover in Mars orbit.

173
00:10:19,673 --> 00:10:22,633
NASA once planned
a similar chain of spacecraft.

174
00:10:23,293 --> 00:10:26,473
The whole mission became
too complex and too expensive.

175
00:10:27,344 --> 00:10:32,224
China plans to bring back
about half a kilogram of Mars, in 2031.

176
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:35,260
Is another space race coming?

177
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:41,520
We've learned to land on Mars,
to drive there, to drill, even to fly.

178
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:44,120
Coming back is a different problem.

179
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:48,160
We know what's waiting on Mars.

180
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:51,140
The question is
who will bring it home first.

181
00:10:53,510 --> 00:10:55,390
So here's a question for you.

182
00:10:55,990 --> 00:10:59,170
If one of those tubes really did
hold signs of ancient life,

183
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:01,510
would it matter which country opened it?

184
00:11:01,890 --> 00:11:03,260
Let me know in the comments.
